# =============================================================================
# Relay H0 MVP — Environment Variables
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values.
# Never commit .env.local to source control.
#
# THERE ARE THREE ENV FILES AND THE SPLIT IS THE SECURITY MODEL. This template is
# for the first one only; the other two are operator identities, not app config:
#
#   .env.local  IAM relay-dev  -> DB relay_dev   read/write product tables, NO DDL,
#                                                cannot write caregiver_leads
#   .env.admin  IAM autospecai -> DB admin       migrations, roles, grants. Holds no
#                                                secret itself, just AWS_PROFILE
#   .env.ro     IAM relay-ro   -> DB relay_ro    SELECT on everything and NOTHING else.
#                                                No writes, no DDL, and NO KMS - so it
#                                                reads metadata and can never decrypt a
#                                                vault. Added 2026-08-21 so verification
#                                                can run somewhere other than one laptop.
#
# !! ALL THREE POINT AT PRODUCTION. There is no dev database. A local run against any
# of them is a production action. See CLAUDE.md 'Environment' and
# docs/secret-rotation-runbook.md.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Aurora DSQL — Multi-Region Active-Active
# Generated by scripts/provision-dsql.sh
# See docs/aws-setup.md for provisioning instructions.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
DSQL_PRIMARY_ENDPOINT=
DSQL_SECONDARY_ENDPOINT=
# Written by scripts/provision-dsql.sh and pushed by scripts/go-live.sh. The
# APPLICATION never reads it — kept because those scripts do.
DSQL_CLUSTER_ARN=

# Set to "true" to route all DB traffic to the secondary region (failover demo)
DSQL_USE_SECONDARY=

# 🔴 WHICH DATABASE ROLE TO CONNECT AS. Unset means `admin`, which is how this
# ran until 2026-08-15 and is still how production runs.
#
# That default is not a recommendation. Until this variable existed, relay had
# exactly one credential and it was root: the app minted an ADMIN auth token, so
# the live product connected with full DDL rights (it could DROP TABLE), and the
# IAM user holding that power was the SAME user whose key sat in .env.local on a
# laptop. Nothing at any layer could tell the deployed product from a laptop.
#
# Set it to a custom role — `relay_dev` locally — and the app mints a NON-admin
# token instead. That is a wall, not a preference: an IAM principal with only
# `dsql:DbConnect` cannot authenticate as admin even if the code asks.
#
# `relay_dev` (migration 030) can read and write the product's tables, holds NO
# DDL rights, and is REVOKEd from writing `caregiver_leads` — the G1 measurement,
# whose only value is that it is arms-length, so a laptop must not be able to
# manufacture demand. A refused write surfaces as 500/CaptureRefused, never as a
# quiet success.
#
# Sysadmin work (migrations, roles) uses a SEPARATE .env.admin with no DSQL_ROLE,
# so acting as an administrator is something you choose rather than carry.
DSQL_ROLE=

# 🔴 TLS CERTIFICATE VERIFICATION. Defaults to ON when unset, which is what
# production wants and what both endpoints were proven to support (their certs
# chain to "Amazon RSA 2048 M04", already in Node's root store).
#
# Set to exactly "false" ONLY as an emergency rollback: it makes the app accept
# any certificate on the connection that carries every ciphertext, wrapped data
# key and IAM auth token. It exists so a TLS incident can be closed in under a
# minute without a redeploy, not as a local convenience.
#
# Read by BOTH lib/db/connection.ts (every request) and db/migrations/migrate.ts.
DSQL_SSL_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=

# --- Migration runner only (db/migrations/migrate.ts) ---
# The app derives these: it always uses port 5432, database "postgres", user
# "admin", and mints a short-lived IAM auth token per connection rather than
# using a password. The migration runner takes them explicitly.
DSQL_PORT=5432
DSQL_DATABASE=postgres
DSQL_USER=admin
# A short-lived IAM auth token, NOT a stored password. Generate with:
#   aws dsql generate-db-connect-auth-token --hostname $DSQL_PRIMARY_ENDPOINT --region us-east-1
DSQL_PASSWORD=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS KMS — Envelope Encryption
# The CMK used to wrap per-item AES-GCM-256 data keys.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
KMS_KEY_ID=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠️ ROTATING ANY SECRET IN THIS FILE HAS A BLAST RADIUS, and for two of them it
# reaches a family in an emergency. docs/secret-rotation-runbook.md has one
# section per secret: what it protects, what breaks mid-flight, the safe order,
# and how to verify afterwards. Read the row before you change the value.
#
# NextAuth.js secret — generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Signs owner sessions (24h), step-up elevation, WebAuthn challenges and
# recovery enrolment. Rotating it signs out every owner — deliberate during an
# incident, a self-inflicted outage otherwise. Runbook §1, which also carries
# the AUTH_SECRET two-names trap.
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=

# Accepted as a fallback wherever NEXTAUTH_SECRET is read (auth-options, signup,
# webauthn). Set ONE of the two. Documented because the fallback is real code —
# an environment that sets only this one works, and nothing said so.
AUTH_SECRET=

# The canonical public origin, preferred over NEXTAUTH_URL when building emailed
# links and as the WebAuthn relying-party origin. Set it to the custom domain in
# production; leaving it blank falls back to NEXTAUTH_URL.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# 🔴 SET THIS TO YOUR LOCAL ORIGIN, NOT A DEPLOYED ONE. Every link Relay emails
# is built from NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL ?? NEXTAUTH_URL, so whatever sits here ends
# up in real invitations sent from your machine. A developer copy holding a
# deployed hostname was found on 2026-08-13 still pointing at the old
# pre-domain vercel.app address — production was correct, but mail sent while
# testing locally carried a raw vercel.app link, which is indistinguishable
# from phishing to somebody receiving it during an emergency. That exact defect
# was already fixed once in production; this is how it comes back.
NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# JWT secret for recipient scoped tokens (HS256)
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
RECIPIENT_JWT_SECRET=

# JWT secret for verifier scoped confirmation tokens (HS256)
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
VERIFIER_JWT_SECRET=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vercel Cron — Heartbeat evaluator
# Shared secret validated in POST /api/cron/heartbeat
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
CRON_SECRET=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI Agents — OpenAI
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Defaults to a small model when unset. Production pins it explicitly so a
# provider-side default cannot change what the intake agent costs or returns.
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email Delivery — Resend
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_FROM_ADDRESS=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS Credentials (local dev only — use IAM role in production)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# Where replies to notification emails go. The From domain may have no inbound
# MX, in which case this is the ONLY way a reply reaches a human. Optional;
# omitted from the message when blank.
RESEND_REPLY_TO_ADDRESS=

# --- Stripe (SHARED ACCOUNT with report-bridge and skillcrossroads) ---
# Relay's footprint is strictly additive: its own product, price and webhook
# endpoint. Never roll STRIPE_SECRET_KEY — the other two projects use it.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# The annual $119 price created for Relay in that account.
STRIPE_PRICE_RELAY_ANNUAL=
# Relay's OWN webhook signing secret, from Relay's endpoint. Not interchangeable
# with report-bridge's — using theirs here rejects every event.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#
# A RESTRICTED, READ-ONLY key for `npm run verify:stripe` (E1.7). Optional, and
# unset is a legitimate state: the script falls back to the paired Stripe CLI and
# still works from this laptop. What is lost when it is unset is SCHEDULING — the
# CLI session key is a human browser pairing that expires 2026-10-07 (E1.8) and
# cannot run on a CI runner, so without this the billing contract is checked only
# when somebody remembers to check it.
#
# 🔴 A SECRET KEY MUST NOT BE PUT HERE. This reads two objects — Webhook
# Endpoints (read) and Billing Portal (read) — and nothing else. The account is
# shared with report-bridge, skillcrossroads and second-brain, so a key with more
# power than it needs has more blast radius than this repo. If it cannot be minted
# that narrowly, leave this unset and keep the CLI fallback; that is the better
# answer, not the worse one.
STRIPE_READONLY_KEY=

# The commit a LOCAL production build is serving, echoed in the Stripe webhook's
# response body as `build.sha` (E1.1). Optional everywhere except one place.
#
# On Vercel this is unnecessary — VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA is set for you. It exists
# for `next build && next start` on a laptop, which is the setting route 3 of the
# E1-prime proof runs in, and where Vercel's variable does not exist. Unset, the
# marker reads `unknown`, which is honest and useless: the whole point of that run
# is to establish WHICH BUILD answered, after nine deliveries once produced a
# result the source cannot produce and a stale module could not be ruled out.
#
# Set it to the SHA you actually built: RELAY_BUILD_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD)
RELAY_BUILD_SHA=

# --- Operator alerting ---
# Where "somebody hit an error" goes. Optional: unset means incidents are still
# written to the runtime log, nobody is woken up, and nothing throws.
#
# ⚠️ The alert rides Resend, the channel measured broken on 2026-08-11 (Outlook
# files us at SCL 5; a bounced address is muted silently). That is deliberate and
# bounded: the alert is a PUSH convenience on top of a record that already exists
# in Vercel's runtime logs. The log is the record; the email is the tap on the
# shoulder. Swapping in a real error service later is lib/ops/incident.ts alone.
OPS_ALERT_EMAIL=

# 🔴 TWO NAMES, ONE SETTING, and the reason is a real misconfiguration. Until
# 2026-08-13 the code read only OPS_ALERT_ADDRESS while this template — and
# therefore production — said OPS_ALERT_EMAIL. The variable somebody set was
# doing nothing, and the fallback to RESEND_REPLY_TO_ADDRESS meant alerts still
# arrived, so nothing looked wrong. Both names are read now. Set either.
#
# ⚠️ SETTING THIS LOCALLY DOES NOT MAKE YOUR LAPTOP MAIL ANYONE. Since
# 2026-08-15 every ops alert is gated on the environment, not just on this
# address: `next dev` (NODE_ENV=development) and preview deployments
# (VERCEL_ENV=preview) resolve to no address and send nothing, while still
# logging every occurrence to stderr. It was the reverse assumption that broke —
# the guard used to be "nobody configured an address locally", and .env.local is
# a full production env file here, so a dev server spent an afternoon filing
# production incidents. See lib/ops/alert-address.ts.
OPS_ALERT_ADDRESS=

# Where a new caregiver lead is announced. Falls back to RESEND_REPLY_TO_ADDRESS.
# ⚠️ This is the input to the G1 gate — the measurement that decides whether the
# product has a market. Unset with no fallback means a lead is recorded and
# nobody is told.
LEAD_NOTIFY_ADDRESS=

# The yearly price shown on the price surfaces. lib/offer.ts is the single
# definition; this only overrides the displayed number.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PRICE_YEARLY_USD=

# E1.2 route 3 (`npm run verify:e1-route3`) — the captured Stripe event it splices.
# Unset is fine: it defaults to .e1-scratch/captured.json. Point it elsewhere to replay a
# DIFFERENT captured payload. If the file is missing the walk exits 2 (could not look) rather
# than inventing an event, because a fabricated payload proves nothing about a real delivery.
E1_PAYLOAD=.e1-scratch/captured.json

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scripts only — never read by the application
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# scripts/canary.ts — what the production canary probes (defaults to the live site)
CANARY_BASE_URL=
# scripts/verify-iam.ts — the IAM user Vercel authenticates as, whose policy decides
# whether the live site can obtain a DSQL ADMIN token. Defaults to "relay-runtime",
# which is correct for this account; set it only to point the check at a different
# principal. Unset changes nothing. The check itself needs .env.admin, because the
# application's own credentials deliberately cannot read IAM.
RELAY_RUNTIME_IAM_USER=
# scripts/demo-run.ts — a deployment to drive, and an authenticated owner cookie
RELAY_BASE_URL=
RELAY_OWNER_COOKIE=
# scripts/a11y-audit.mjs — which origin to audit, and which page set
A11Y_BASE=
A11Y_SCOPE=
# scripts/a11y-audit.mjs — WHOSE session owner mode is audited in.
#
# 🔴 IT WAS HARDCODED TO `demo@relay.test`, WHICH NO LONGER EXISTS, so owner mode
# silently stopped being audited and every run fell into the loud skip branch —
# reporting 18 pages where CC8 records 21. Unset, it still defaults to that
# address; set it to any account that exists and the eight owner screens are
# covered. `scripts/scratch-owner-for-audit.ts create` makes a disposable one on
# a reserved domain and prints the address to use here.
A11Y_OWNER_EMAIL=
# 🔴 WHO A NON-PRODUCTION SERVER IS ALLOWED TO EMAIL. Comma-separated addresses.
#
# Outside production, `sendEmail` refuses any recipient not named here. Unset
# means a dev or preview server sends NOTHING, which is the safe default.
#
# It exists because .env.local carries the production Resend key and points at
# the production cluster — Relay has no dev database — so `npm run dev` running
# any notification path mailed a real person, from the real sending domain, on
# an account SHARED with report-bridge. The ops-alert path was gated on
# 2026-08-15; this is the one that reaches customers.
#
# ⚠️ An ALLOW-LIST rather than "reserved domains only", because that rule is
# backwards here: RESERVED_TLDS already refuses .test/.invalid/.localhost, so
# permitting only those would permit nothing. Testing mail off production needs
# a real address you control — put it here. The reserved-domain refusal still
# applies on top, so an allow-listed .test address is still refused.
#
# The three E2E walks are unaffected: they assert nothing about mail.
DEV_MAIL_ALLOWLIST=
# scripts/verify-funnel.ts — which origin the G1 instrument check drives.
# Defaults to https://relaystandby.com, because the instrument that matters is
# the one the ads point at. It writes NOTHING to the database (it never submits
# the interest form), so pointing it at production is safe.
FUNNEL_BASE_URL=
# scripts/verify-funnel.ts — the `src` that check walks under. Defaults to `qa`.
#
# 🔴 IT REFUSES ANY src THAT COUNTS TOWARD THE GATE, and that guard is the point
# of the variable existing at all. Driving the funnel under a real lane value
# injects one qualified and one intent at 100% conversion — roughly a full point
# on a 2% ship line at N=100, biasing toward a false PASS. A caregiver_leads row
# can be deleted; a Vercel Analytics event cannot. Only `qa` and `preflight` are
# accepted, and that list is `QA_SRCS` in src/app/caregivers/content.ts — read
# through the same isGateQualifyingSrc() the gate itself uses, so the two answers
# cannot drift apart.
FUNNEL_SRC=
# scripts/scratch-*-e2e.ts — which origin the end-to-end walks drive. Defaults to
# http://localhost:3000. These create and delete disposable accounts, so pointing
# it at a deployment is a real write to that environment.
E2E_BASE=
# lib/notify + /api/resend/webhook — the Svix signing secret for Resend's
# delivery webhook ("whsec_..."), from Resend dashboard -> Webhooks. WITHOUT IT the
# endpoint records nothing and every contact's delivery state reads as unheard —
# which is why the UI says nothing rather than "reachable". Set it and the owner
# finds out in calm that a contact bounced, instead of during the emergency.
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# scripts/guide-pdf.mjs — which origin to print the user's guide from. It must be
# a SERVED origin, never a file:// path: the guide's images are root-absolute, so
# printing the file directly produces a PDF of 26 broken images.
GUIDE_BASE=

# scripts/guide-pdf.mjs — WHICH guide to print, and where the PDF lands. Both
# default to the user's guide (/guide -> public/guide/relay-guide.pdf), so
# leaving them unset prints exactly what it always has. They exist so the
# beta-test guide is printed by the SAME script rather than a copied one:
#   $env:GUIDE_PATH='/guide/beta'; $env:GUIDE_OUT='docs/Relay-Beta-Test-Guide.pdf'
# ⚠️ Set them in PowerShell, not Git Bash — MSYS rewrites a leading-slash value
# into a Windows path and Playwright then fails on a nonsense URL.
GUIDE_PATH=
GUIDE_OUT=
# lib/seed/demo-data.ts — point the demo recipient at a real inbox before
# reseeding if you need to receive its mail
DEMO_RECIPIENT_EMAIL=

# lib/seed/demo-data.ts — the inbox seeded CONTACTS are sub-addressed from.
# Defaults to the maintainer's; set it to your own before reseeding so demo mail
# lands somewhere you can read. Never a reserved domain: @example.com hard-bounces
# on a shared sender, which is how four seeded contacts came to be undeliverable.
DEMO_CONTACT_INBOX=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Retired — deliberately absent
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TOTP_SECRET was a single shared second factor, kept as a fallback for accounts
# with no secret of their own. It was RETIRED on 2026-08-13: the fallback was
# described as covering pre-signup accounts and actually covered every standby
# contact ever created, making one env var a master key over a growing
# population. Nothing reads it now (lib/auth/resolve-totp-secret.ts, and a test
# asserts the file touches no environment variable at all).
#
# It is named here rather than silently dropped so that anyone who finds it still
# set in a deployment knows it is inert and can remove it — an env var whose
# meaning has changed is worse than one that never existed.
